WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.747
Passwords remain common because they are familiar and easy to deploy, but they carry predictable risks.

2
00:00:06.747 --> 00:00:14.797
Users may choose weak passwords, reuse passwords across services, store them insecurely, or reveal them to impostor sites.

3
00:00:14.797 --> 00:00:19.628
If one service is compromised, reused credentials may be tried elsewhere.

4
00:00:19.628 --> 00:00:29.211
Brute-force resistance at a high level depends on password strength, throttling, rate limits, detection, and the quality of server-side storage.

5
00:00:29.211 --> 00:00:36.112
Password managers can improve real-world behavior by helping users generate and store unique passwords.

6
00:00:36.112 --> 00:00:41.632
Password policy should balance security, usability, and realistic human behavior.

7
00:00:41.632 --> 00:00:51.216
Rules that force frequent arbitrary changes or complicated patterns can backfire if users respond with predictable variations or unsafe storage.

8
00:00:51.216 --> 00:00:59.113
Better policy encourages length, uniqueness, blocklists for known bad passwords, and support for secure storage tools.

9
00:00:59.113 --> 00:01:07.700
Secure password storage means the system should not store plaintext passwords or reversible values intended for routine recovery.

10
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:15.903
Salted password hashing stores password verifiers in a way that resists direct plaintext recovery if the database is exposed.

11
00:01:15.903 --> 00:01:24.567
The salt helps prevent identical passwords from producing identical stored values and makes large precomputed attacks less useful.

12
00:01:24.567 --> 00:01:30.317
Password hashing parameters and upgrade paths should be owned and maintained over time.

13
00:01:30.317 --> 00:01:33.998
Password reset flows are part of credential protection.

14
00:01:33.998 --> 00:01:46.111
A strong storage design can still be undermined by weak recovery, predictable reset links, excessive information disclosure, or help desk processes that cannot resist social pressure.

15
00:01:46.111 --> 00:01:57.842
Rate limiting, lockout, and throttling also involve tradeoffs: too little control invites guessing, while aggressive lockout can create denial-of-service or support problems.

16
00:01:57.842 --> 00:02:01.215
Good password design considers the full lifecycle.
